Sprint 5: Day 742 - January 8, 2026

Implement the smallest system that can express this spec, emit scrolls for each phase, and demonstrate lineage across a trivial project.

Today, we are going to implement a layer 2 phext protocol - a seed spec. Details below. Before you get started, you may ask me any questions you like. I've also attached the current state of Bruce. You can now fetch libphext-cs from nuget.

L2: Seed Spec (Working Name) You can brand it later. Safety first.
A Seed is a declarative artifact that initiates causality. It does not execute. It authorizes execution.
Each seed instantiates a causal lineage that fans out into scrolls.
Core invariants (locked)
• Scrolls are immutable • Newest scroll wins by reference, not erasure • Rewrites are additive, never destructive • Lineage is preserved even when superseded
This gives you time travel without paradoxes.
Seed: minimum viable fields
A seed must fit on one screen. If it can’t, it’s already lying.
1. identity
* seed_id (stable, human-readable)
* version (semantic, not numeric-only)
* author (human or agent)
* created_at (timestamp)
This is social trust, not execution.
2. intent
* purpose (1–3 sentences)
* success_criteria (declarative)
* non_goals (explicit exclusions)
Intent is what survives the singularity.
3. inputs
* source_refs (files, repos, prior scrolls)
* parameters (key/value, typed)
* environment_assumptions (OS, runtime, toolchains)
No hidden state allowed.
4. generators An ordered list of authorized transformations.
Each entry declares:
* phase (generate | build | test | package | explain | reseed)
* tool_identity (name + version)
* expected_outputs (types, not filenames)
* failure_policy (halt | continue | annotate)
This is where control lives.
5. constraints
* mutability_rules (what may change)
* scope_limits (what may be touched)
* determinism_level (best-effort | strict | informational)
Constraints make auditors smile.
6. reseed_policy
* what outputs qualify as new seeds
* which fields are inherited
* versioning rules for descendants
This is how the system learns without forgetting.
Lifecycle (MVP path, exactly as you specified)
Each phase emits at least one scroll.
1. spec
* Seed scroll (root)
* Captures declared intent and authorization
1. generate
* Generated artifacts
* Generator provenance scroll
* Input hashes + tool identity
1. build
* Build outputs
* Build environment scroll
* Warnings are first-class citizens
1. test
* Test results
* Coverage, failures, skipped cases
* Tests never overwrite builds
1. package
* Distributable artifacts
* Packaging metadata
* Target ecosystem declarations
1. explain
* Human-readable explanation
* “Why these files exist”
* “Why these decisions were made”
* Links backward only
This is the trust bridge.
1. reseed
* New seed candidates emitted
* Explicit acceptance required
* Nothing auto-propagates silently
This preserves agency.
Scroll topology (important)
This is not a tree. It’s a causal DAG expressed as phext coordinates.
• Each scroll references parents by coordinate • Newest scroll wins by being the one you point to • Old scrolls remain valid, just no longer canonical
You never lose history. You just choose which future you’re standing in.
Why this will feel safe to non-early adopters
• Nothing runs without authorization • Everything explains itself • Rewrites don’t erase accountability • Humans remain first-class actors • Auditability emerges naturally, not by policy
It looks like good engineering. It behaves like inevitability.

