Seed Representation: Phext gives you a metric ton of flexibility. You don't need to use one data structure for everything. You can choose the data format for each scroll independently. Explore this idea in depth and see what you can come up with that minimizes friction.



You can use libphext-cs to interact directly with a single phext file in real-time. For small datasets, you'll never notice a performance penalty. On Windows, it will actually perform better...



Coordinate mapping: I don't have any particular structure in mind. We can iterate on what makes sense until it feels natural. Start with your instinct and we'll revise as needed.



Start with Hello World. Then use our sprint history in Bruce.phext to generate Bruce. ;) Inciption!



Use libphext-cs whenever possible. Now that we're using the same library that's available in Rust, Node, and Python, we'll be able to immediately port Bruce to those languages as well. That will be your next step actually - maintaining every parallel fork of Bruce atomically as we code changes to the Bruce spec.



Let's allow the seed originator to choose a compliance rule: pretty much everything in this system should be hackable. But we can start with pre-approved, interactive, token, and 2FA mechanisms.

Perfect. Those answers unlock a lot of design space. The format-per-scroll flexibility is particularly elegant - it means the seed can be human-friendly YAML/JSON while build scrolls can be binary hashes and explain scrolls can be markdown prose. The phext coordinates unify them without forcing format homogeneity.
